\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Passage Commentary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Mark 10:1-12 | Jesus Confronts Hardness Of Heart And Protects Covenant Faithfulness\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Jesus goes into Judea and across the Jordan, and again crowds come to him (v. 1). As usual, he teaches them. But the Pharisees come with a test: "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife?" (v. 2, NIV). They are not asking as wounded people seeking wisdom. Mark says they are testing him. They want to trap Jesus inside a legal dispute.\
Jesus answers by asking what Moses commanded them, and they point to the certificate of divorce permitted in the law (vv. 3-4). Jesus does not deny that Moses allowed it. But he names why it was allowed: "It was because your hearts were hard that Moses wrote you this law" (v. 5, NIV). Permission in a broken world is not the same as God's creational desire.\
So Jesus goes behind the legal permission to God's beginning: "But at the beginning of creation God 'made them male and female.' 'For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and the two will become one flesh'" (vv. 6-8, NIV). Marriage is not treated as a disposable arrangement governed by convenience or male privilege. It is a one-flesh covenant under God.\
"Therefore what God has joined together, let no one separate" (v. 9, NIV). Jesus' words are weighty because marriage matters. Covenant faithfulness matters. Human hardness can wound what God intended for faithful union, protection, and love. In that setting, divorce could leave women especially vulnerable, and Jesus refuses to let a legal question hide the damage done by hardened hearts.\
This passage must be handled with pastoral care. Jesus is not giving abusers permission to trap victims in danger. He is not handing a weapon to the self-righteous so they can shame every wounded person. He is confronting the hardness of heart that treats covenant as disposable and people as manageable. The church must honor Jesus' seriousness about marriage while also protecting the endangered, caring for the abandoned, and refusing to use his words cruelly against the wounded.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 10:13-16 | Jesus Welcomes Children And Teaches Kingdom Reception\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 People bring little children to Jesus so that he might place his hands on them, but the disciples rebuke them (v. 13). This is jarring because Jesus has recently placed a child among the disciples to teach them about receiving the low-status in his name. Yet the disciples still act as gatekeepers. They decide who is important enough to get near Jesus.\
When Jesus sees this, he is indignant (v. 14). Mark does not often tell us Jesus' emotional reaction so directly. He is angry because the disciples are blocking the very people who display the posture of the kingdom. "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these" (v. 14, NIV).\
Children in this scene are not romantic symbols of innocence. They are dependent, low-status, and unable to leverage power. They come receiving. Jesus says that is how the kingdom must be received: "Anyone who will not receive the kingdom of God like a little child will never enter it" (v. 15, NIV). The kingdom is not seized by the impressive. It is received by the empty-handed.\
Then Jesus takes the children in his arms, places his hands on them, and blesses them (v. 16). This is the embodied answer to the disciples' rebuke. Jesus does not merely tolerate the children. He receives them warmly. He gives them time, touch, attention, and blessing.\
That scene should re-form the church's instincts. The people we are tempted to treat as interruptions may be the very people through whom Jesus teaches us the kingdom. A community that belongs to Jesus cannot measure access by usefulness, maturity, wealth, age, polish, or importance. Jesus opens his arms to the ones others push aside.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 10:17-22 | Jesus Loves The Rich Man And Names His Captivity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 As Jesus starts on his way, a man runs up, falls on his knees, and asks, "Good teacher, what must I do to inherit eternal life?" (v. 17, NIV). He is earnest. He is respectful. He is not casual about spiritual things. But his question still turns on something he can do.\
Jesus first directs him to the commandments: do not murder, commit adultery, steal, give false testimony, defraud, and honor father and mother (v. 19). The man says he has kept all these since he was a boy (v. 20). We should not rush to sneer at him. He appears morally serious. He has tried to obey. But moral seriousness does not mean the heart is free.\
Then Mark gives us one of the most beautiful and painful sentences in the passage: "Jesus looked at him and loved him" (v. 21, NIV). What Jesus says next is not loveless severity. It is love telling the truth. "One thing you lack," Jesus says. "Go, sell everything you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me" (v. 21, NIV).\
Jesus names the rival treasure. The man has many commandments in place, but his wealth still holds the center. Jesus does not ask him to add one more religious achievement. He asks him to release the thing that owns him and follow. The call is concrete: sell, give to the poor, have treasure in heaven, come, follow.\
The man goes away sad because he has great wealth (v. 22). His sorrow shows that Jesus has touched the true nerve. He wants eternal life, but he also wants to keep the life his wealth secures. He wants Jesus, but not more than he wants his possessions. This is why the rich man is not only a distant example. He is a mirror. We may not all have his wealth, but we all know what it is to grieve when Jesus touches what we cannot imagine releasing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 10:23-27 | Jesus Exposes Wealth's Danger And God's Possibility\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Jesus looks around and says, "How hard it is for the rich to enter the kingdom of God!" (v. 23, NIV). The disciples are amazed, so Jesus says it again with even stronger imagery: it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone rich to enter the kingdom of God (v. 25).\
The disciples are even more amazed and ask, "Who then can be saved?" (v. 26, NIV). Their question reveals their assumptions. If the wealthy, moral, serious, and respected cannot easily enter, then who can? Wealth often looks like security, blessing, competence, and importance. Jesus says it can become a spiritual danger precisely because it teaches the soul to trust what it has.\
Jesus does not answer by lowering the demand. He says, "With man this is impossible, but not with God; all things are possible with God" (v. 27, NIV). Salvation is not difficult in the sense that we need slightly more effort. It is impossible by human power. No one can detach their heart from its idols, make themselves childlike, undo hardness of heart, or purchase eternal life.\
But what is impossible with us is possible with God. That is not permission to keep our idols. It is hope that God can free us from them. The rich can be saved, but not by wealth. The moral can be saved, but not by morality. The religious can be saved, but not by religious seriousness. The kingdom is received by grace, and grace is powerful enough to loosen even the grip of great wealth.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Mark 10:28-31 | Jesus Promises Kingdom Family With Persecutions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Peter begins to say, "We have left everything to follow you!" (v. 28, NIV). There is a real question underneath his statement. If the rich man is called to leave his possessions, and the disciples have left much already, what becomes of their loss?\
Jesus answers with a promise. No one who has left home, brothers, sisters, mother, father, children, or fields for Jesus and the gospel will fail to receive a hundred times as much in this present age: homes, brothers, sisters, mothers, children, and fields, along with persecutions, and in the age to come eternal life (vv. 29-30).\
This promise is not a prosperity formula. Jesus includes persecutions. Following him does not mean loss disappears. It means loss is gathered into a new kingdom reality. Jesus creates a family around himself. Those who lose home or kinship or security for him and the gospel are not unseen. The church is meant to become a real household of care, hospitality, belonging, and shared life for those who follow Jesus at cost.\
Then Jesus ends with reversal: "But many who are first will be last, and the last first" (v. 31, NIV). That sentence gathers the whole passage. Men with legal power are not first because they can manage divorce. Adults are not first because they can block children. The wealthy are not first because they possess much. The disciples are not first because they left more than others. The kingdom belongs to those who receive, release, follow, and trust the God who makes the impossible possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Mark 10:1-31 In One Movement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 Mark 10:1-31 shows Jesus dismantling the ways people try to control life. The Pharisees use a divorce question to test him, but Jesus exposes hardness of heart and returns to God's faithful purpose for marriage. The disciples try to manage Jesus' time by rebuking children, but Jesus is indignant and says the kingdom belongs to such as these. A rich man asks what he must do to inherit eternal life, but Jesus lovingly names the wealth that owns him, and the man walks away sad. The disciples are astonished because Jesus has made salvation look impossible. And Jesus agrees: with humans it is impossible, but with God all things are possible. The kingdom is not controlled by legal power, adult importance, moral performance, or wealth. It is received with empty hands, followed with costly trust, and given by the God who makes the first last and the last first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Scripture Cross-References\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Genesis 1:27; Genesis 2:24 | Jesus grounds marriage in God's creation of male and female and the one-flesh union.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Deuteronomy 24:1-4 | Moses' divorce concession stands behind the Pharisees' question and Jesus' contrast with hardness of heart.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Mark 9:36-37 | Jesus had already centered a child to teach the disciples about receiving the low-status in his name.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Exodus 20:12-16 | Jesus names commandments from the law in his conversation with the rich man.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Matthew 6:19-24 | Jesus' teaching on treasure and serving God rather than money helps interpret the rich man's captivity.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Acts 2:44-47 | The early church gives a glimpse of kingdom family and shared life around Jesus.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Shape of a Kingdom People\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 A kingdom people protect covenant faithfulness without weaponizing Jesus' words against the wounded. Jesus takes marriage seriously because people are not disposable. But his words must be used in the spirit of his kingdom, not as tools of shame or control. The church should resist hardness of heart, protect the vulnerable, care for the abandoned, and honor the faithful love God intended.\
A kingdom people make room for children and all who come empty-handed. The disciples think they are protecting Jesus from interruption, but Jesus is indignant because they are blocking those to whom the kingdom belongs. The church should be very careful when efficiency, seriousness, polish, or adult importance starts pushing the vulnerable away from Jesus.\
A kingdom people tell the truth about wealth. Jesus does not treat the rich man's possessions as a small spiritual detail. He tells him to sell, give to the poor, and follow. Money is not only a private heart issue; it shapes relationships, possibilities, power, and allegiance. The kingdom loosens our grip so that treasure can move toward the poor and our lives can move after Jesus.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Gospel Shaped Reflection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 From this passage we learn to receive before we achieve. Jesus does not invite us to control the kingdom through law, status, morality, or possessions. He exposes hardness, welcomes children, loves the rich man enough to tell him the truth, and declares that what is impossible for us is possible with God. Our response begins with empty hands before the God who gives grace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Who is God?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God is faithful to his creational purposes and does not treat people as disposable (vv. 6-9).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God sees and confronts hardness of heart (v. 5).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God welcomes children and gives the kingdom to those who receive it with empty hands (vv. 14-15).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God loves us enough to name the treasure that owns us (v. 21).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God can do what is impossible for human beings: save, free, and re-create us (v. 27).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}God forms a new kingdom family for those who follow Jesus at cost (vv. 29-30).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 What has God done?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has redirected a test about divorce toward God's deeper purpose for covenant faithfulness (vv. 2-9).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has received children in his arms, placed his hands on them, and blessed them (v. 16).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has looked at the rich man with love and exposed his captivity to wealth (v. 21).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has declared that salvation is impossible with humans but possible with God (v. 27).\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Jesus has promised present kingdom family, persecution, eternal life, and final reversal (vv. 29-31).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 Who are we in light of what God has done?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are not controllers of the kingdom; we are receivers of grace.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are people whose hardness of heart must be brought into the light before Jesus.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are called to welcome children and vulnerable people rather than block their way to Jesus.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are not saved by morality, money, sacrifice, or religious seriousness.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are loved by Jesus even when he names the thing we do not want to release.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We are members of a kingdom family where the first become last and the last first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 What wrong behavior, harmful emotions, or false attitudes result when we forget these truths?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We use rules to protect control rather than to seek God's heart.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We treat relationships as disposable when they become costly.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We block inconvenient people from Jesus because they disrupt our sense of importance or order.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We assume moral seriousness means our hearts are free.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We cling to money, status, property, or security as if they can give us eternal life.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We spiritualize wealth so we do not have to ask what Jesus may be calling us to release for the poor.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We resent costly discipleship when we forget the kingdom family Jesus gives.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 How would we live if we believed these truths?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would bring our hardness of heart to Jesus instead of hiding it behind technical questions.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would honor marriage and covenant faithfulness while caring tenderly for wounded and endangered people.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would welcome children, the vulnerable, and the overlooked as people who belong near Jesus.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would ask what treasure has become too precious for us to surrender.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would practice generosity that moves toward the poor, not merely inward detachment from money.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would follow Jesus with the confidence that what he asks us to release is not greater than what he gives.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We would receive the kingdom like children: dependent, open-handed, and trusting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb260\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 If we lived this out, how would it impact our neighborhood, family, church, workplace, and friends?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our homes would become places where covenant faithfulness is honored and wounded people are cared for with truth and tenderness.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our churches would become safer and warmer for children, single parents, divorced people, the poor, and those who feel spiritually unimpressive.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our friendships would become places where we can name the treasures that compete with Jesus without pretending.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our workplaces would see people less controlled by money, advancement, and security because our future is held by God.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our neighborhoods would encounter Christians whose generosity is concrete enough to bless the poor.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Our shared life would become a foretaste of kingdom family: homes opened, resources shared, losses noticed, persecutions endured, and the last honored as first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Hearing the Father's Voice\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs23 \cf0 The Bible is God's love letter to us. When we read it, we do not want accusation, fear, shame, or distance to distort what God has spoken. In this passage, the Father shows us that his kingdom is not seized by control, importance, morality, or wealth, but received from his generous hand. The words that follow are written to help us hear the Father's love through the passage we have just studied.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa160\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 My child, come to me with empty hands. You do not have to make yourself important before I bless you, and you do not have to hide the places where your heart has grown hard. My Son looks at you with love, and his love is strong enough to tell you the truth.\
Let go of what cannot save you. Receive the kingdom like a child. I am not taking from you to leave you empty; I am freeing your hands for life, for mercy, for the poor, for my family, and for my Son. What is impossible for you is possible with me, and in my kingdom the last are not forgotten. They are brought home.\
}